carts:
The stubble's joints between;
And carts went stooping round the fields
To take the pumpkins in.

carry:
To chalk its height on, wears a dog-chain round
Its regal neck, and learns to carry and fetch
The fashions of the day to please the day;

carpets:
The cabin changed to a stately home,
Rich carpets were hushing her tread;
But light was fading from her eye,

cares:
With a smile to warm and the tears to refresh us;
And joy seems sweeter when cares come after,
And a moan is the finest of foils for laughter;

careers:
That rambles in the road alone,
And doesn't care about careers,
And exigencies never fears;

carol:
When he beats his bars and he would be free;
It is not a carol of joy or glee,
But a prayer that he sends from his heart's deep core,

carcass:
Wept you, oh, wept you, alone by the river,
When my stark carcass you secretly sank.
Ha, now I see that you tremble and shiver;

carnage:
Sweeping round each war-crushed heart;
Men to say to strife and carnage--
From our world henceforth depart.

carolling:
As goodly a Prince as ever was born;
Carolling with the carolling lark;--
Sure his bride will be won and worn,

carnivorous:
To get at men excepting through their souls,
However open their carnivorous jaws;
And poets get directlier at the soul,
